[{:db/id "q-78978/selection/05402db46107d70ece210f0bd5e24b332ae4f43bd474d592035c3e3c92625629" :answer/id #uuid "764f530d-bb60-588c-939b-5abda662f2fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78978/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "099264be-d27a-52a3-b7c1-618a09a89ace" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78978/selection/6ee96c99c6020a8a0675f054530cea7b5db72506dc41f7e364f7485717e0d433" :answer/id #uuid "32acc7fd-753d-5c0b-b3b3-9cca6fcd245b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x)$ does not have any local minima" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78978/selection/db7b536abbfbcf7bf94ee6f7b1bfcfcde80a4b0681c0d4a814e23a1769d0dc6f" :answer/id #uuid "d4253e07-4a40-54b8-9d21-8780beb30e1e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78978/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "69a8852c-c2bd-5a22-a1e5-dba442b0048b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78978/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c3b728e9-61ec-57ad-8327-50147b359e53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-78978/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-78978/selection/05402db46107d70ece210f0bd5e24b332ae4f43bd474d592035c3e3c92625629" "q-78978/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-78978/selection/6ee96c99c6020a8a0675f054530cea7b5db72506dc41f7e364f7485717e0d433" "q-78978/selection/db7b536abbfbcf7bf94ee6f7b1bfcfcde80a4b0681c0d4a814e23a1769d0dc6f" "q-78978/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-78978" :question/id #uuid "f31a2fb0-549c-5b45-aecb-95fd4bb53cca" :question/math-academy-id "q-78978" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-12x+4$ have a local minimum?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-12 \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n3({x}^{2}-4)=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=\\pm 2.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-3,0,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-3) & =3((-3)^{2}-4)=15>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3((0)^{2}-4)=-12<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =3((3)^{2}-4)=15>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-3$ |  | $0$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-2$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=2,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=2$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local minimum at $x=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-78978/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33965139-0c6a-50fc-993a-e2acd6760212"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-78978"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-78981/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "05d103eb-32b0-549f-bbb3-20fa5fd4ab36" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78981/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "60b46815-e6aa-527e-b849-51f77da74e15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78981/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "38a7db3d-e7ea-5319-9125-389425cddb14" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78981/selection/74053cd8f212994373f56db80fbf0eade2779b20bfbeb0bc87c25a146cc918d6" :answer/id #uuid "bea020b8-3d45-5f93-a83d-f89b5bc32115" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x)$ does not have any local maxima" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78981/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "e45afc05-cba4-59f6-9ba3-75857542da54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78981/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e0d87964-b4a7-5b87-bafb-b78beceb61b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-78981/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-78981/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-78981/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-78981/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" "q-78981/selection/74053cd8f212994373f56db80fbf0eade2779b20bfbeb0bc87c25a146cc918d6" "q-78981/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-78981" :question/id #uuid "4bb06435-9175-533a-8727-5e983d5d29c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-78981" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=18x-2{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}$ have a local maximum?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =18-6{x}^{2}-12x \\\\  & =-6({x}^{2}+2x-3) \\\\  & =-6(x-1)(x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-6(x-1)(x+3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3,1.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=-3$ and $x=1.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-4,0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-4) & =-6(-4-1)(-4+3)=-30<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =-6(0-1)(0+3)=18>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =-6(2-1)(2+3)=-30<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $-3$ | $(-3,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-4$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-3,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-3$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=1,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=1$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local maximum at $x=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-78981/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33965139-0c6a-50fc-993a-e2acd6760212"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-78981"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-113756/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "53b286b2-66ec-53b1-ac05-83e6a59eda28" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113756/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "a79408c7-418d-5ad5-a434-cc69fc488420" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113756/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "8ef8bdae-4107-518d-bd08-5f79ecff4ee1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113756/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "c7ea4f71-8380-5b9c-a8d2-9ec15242eca3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113756/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "873be0bc-2e26-5781-8a45-35c040b74fb0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113756/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e1c80fcd-2da6-5e86-a5fc-3f639fbba1a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-113756/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-113756/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-113756/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-113756/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-113756/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-113756/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-113756" :question/id #uuid "dfddadf1-89e7-55ef-9cec-6665abaa08ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-113756" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+4,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ is a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is a local maximum\n- $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is a local minimum" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}-2x \\\\  & =2x(3x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(3x-1)\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,\\frac{1}{4},1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =2(-1)(3(-1)-1)=8>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-\\frac{1}{4}) & =2(\\frac{1}{4})[3(\\frac{1}{4})-1]=-\\frac{1}{8}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =2(1)(3(1)-1)=4>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\frac{1}{3})$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $(\\frac{1}{3},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{1}{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is a local minimum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-113756/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33965139-0c6a-50fc-993a-e2acd6760212"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-113756"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-22820/selection/74053cd8f212994373f56db80fbf0eade2779b20bfbeb0bc87c25a146cc918d6" :answer/id #uuid "e9d5fa21-a465-5364-991e-0c1ca4c3d027" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x)$ does not have any local maxima" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-22820/selection/0d474ca756c76f6ecddcd5fcfb411f0292ef0ce725af778ee27af0d5dc8595bc" :answer/id #uuid "d19cc507-1717-5bc1-b722-d7cf73bcca07" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-22820/selection/de2f9e8d93e24093f903d192e87652a275278440944fbfbf925ef2d61e21dc26" :answer/id #uuid "886178e6-0d6a-55f2-b95c-bbcae5ac7e6a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-22820/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "bf465f11-4498-53b1-b90c-1c7909139642" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-22820/selection/a2301b8122c312044fb5dc0b0c60c599b4f2f7f4ffcc198c9893873ab9165c9d" :answer/id #uuid "747056ce-e799-5d02-8c4d-a27a1724760e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-22820/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "48aa4abd-b180-5e8e-9816-3c7c5a88ffe0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-22820/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-22820/selection/74053cd8f212994373f56db80fbf0eade2779b20bfbeb0bc87c25a146cc918d6" "q-22820/selection/0d474ca756c76f6ecddcd5fcfb411f0292ef0ce725af778ee27af0d5dc8595bc" "q-22820/selection/de2f9e8d93e24093f903d192e87652a275278440944fbfbf925ef2d61e21dc26" "q-22820/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-22820/selection/a2301b8122c312044fb5dc0b0c60c599b4f2f7f4ffcc198c9893873ab9165c9d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-22820" :question/id #uuid "5fc8da11-49c7-5976-a449-f66fccf4a77a" :question/math-academy-id "q-22820" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)={e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}$ have a local maximum?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (2x){e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}=-x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2} & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-(-1){e}^{-(-1)^{2}/2}={e}^{-1/2}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-(1){e}^{-(1)^{2}/2}=-{e}^{-1/2}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-22820/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-22820"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-49027/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "eaacdb70-8962-559e-b86f-73f541e4e78b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49027/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "ab372549-5e55-5f0f-8680-d7f806f640b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49027/selection/74053cd8f212994373f56db80fbf0eade2779b20bfbeb0bc87c25a146cc918d6" :answer/id #uuid "ee23f4f2-8fbd-5b6b-886e-0585b24ff681" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x)$ does not have any local maxima" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49027/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "15acbacd-80be-5b38-ab6d-978ae37832a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49027/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "aa3df24e-7a41-5725-81c1-da1429730b8f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49027/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8ac012a5-54c1-5929-9ba0-89212801f42f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-49027/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-49027/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-49027/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-49027/selection/74053cd8f212994373f56db80fbf0eade2779b20bfbeb0bc87c25a146cc918d6" "q-49027/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-49027/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-49027" :question/id #uuid "d7fa8c48-a61b-5fbf-8ae6-af7bb5265a7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-49027" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{1+x}$ defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ have a local maximum?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})\\cdot (1+x)-\\sqrt{x}}{(1+x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1+x)-2x}{2\\sqrt{x}(1+x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-x}{2\\sqrt{x}(1+x)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ on $(0,\\infty )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-x}{2\\sqrt{x}(1+x)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 1-x & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=1$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=\\frac{1}{2},2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{(1-\\frac{1}{2})}{2\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}{(1+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{9\\sqrt{2}}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1-2}{2\\sqrt{2}(1+2)^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{18\\sqrt{2}}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x=1,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=1$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-49027/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-49027"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344401/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "64d77bf0-76e9-5323-b000-308d4373b0e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344401/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "5b5506ca-5ab5-5e87-846b-480bc7ad94fa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344401/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-344401/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344401" :question/id #uuid "6bd80546-8633-537d-bf3f-1c4166a0651b" :question/math-academy-id "q-344401" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-{x}^{2}$ have a local maximum? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe local maximum is at $x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}-2x\\,=\\,2x(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}-1)\\,=\\,-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}+1} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-\\frac{2(-1)^{3}}{(-1)^{2}+1}=\\frac{2}{2}=1>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-\\frac{2(1)^{3}}{(1)^{2}+1}=-\\frac{2}{2}=-1<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-344401/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344401"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-21468/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "d65837df-abbd-5d68-9419-2938a2f9c7f5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21468/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "21698a41-de36-5785-a4f5-78217017f9d8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21468/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "ded1075a-3627-5632-b6c8-c6e06d4f54af" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21468/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "0beba30f-0a04-54c7-9fc0-5846abd439d6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21468/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "fb3fb868-4570-5a9a-b52a-747182db6646" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21468/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e4e0a153-f6a8-5035-b9a0-6164963a381e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-21468/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-21468/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-21468/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-21468/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-21468/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-21468/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-21468" :question/id #uuid "2f415a64-6204-50b1-8aee-a5b869400010" :question/math-academy-id "q-21468" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x)=4{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+9,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-\\frac{3}{4}$ is a local minimum\n- $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is a local minimum" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=16{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}=4{x}^{2}(4x+3).\n$$\n\nAnd now find the stationary points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}(4x+3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0\\,\\text{or}\\,x=-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=-\\frac{3}{4},\\,0.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,-\\frac{1}{2},1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =4(-1)^{2}(4(-1)+3)=-4<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-\\frac{1}{2}) & =4{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}(4(-\\frac{1}{2})+3)=1>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =4(1)^{2}(4+3)=28>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{3}{4})$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $(-\\frac{3}{4},0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\frac{3}{4},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-\\frac{3}{4}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative does not change the sign. Therefore, $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-21468/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff7c597d-d4a4-5567-add6-eac77475b0d6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-21468"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-49026/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "3662e3ac-6963-5cdf-9a68-50464e2afddd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49026/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "23fcb0b0-3ffe-5ac2-aca0-fed04d4e131d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49026/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "336891a4-d430-5c42-8084-cc47188b48a0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49026/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "015fc6fa-d299-5fc7-8189-f2e1ec50b962" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49026/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "a5c056e3-7378-55ef-a1a4-6b037380aa3c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49026/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3440ced2-8f43-5053-bc60-f826dfc15ccc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-49026/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-49026/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-49026/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-49026/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-49026/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-49026/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-49026" :question/id #uuid "f0d38c8a-0688-526d-84c1-a9ace67f2eb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-49026" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{e}^{x}},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=0$ is a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum\n- $x=3$ is a local minimum" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3{x}^{2}{e}^{x}-{x}^{3}{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}=\\frac{3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}}{{e}^{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}}{{e}^{x}} & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(3-x) & =0 \\\\ x=0,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0,\\,3.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1,4$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{3(-1)^{2}-(-1)^{3}}{{e}^{-1}}=4e>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{3(1)^{2}-(1)^{3}}{{e}^{1}}=\\frac{2}{e}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{3(4)^{2}-(4)^{3}}{{e}^{4}}=-\\frac{16}{{e}^{4}}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |  | $4$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative does not change the sign. Therefore, $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=3,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=3$ is a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-49026/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff7c597d-d4a4-5567-add6-eac77475b0d6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-49026"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-113767/selection/d9b70cb012b8a98348e0eb2b076c0ffaa03d2f9b5ed0b778c333cec60818df33" :answer/id #uuid "9020d897-268f-501e-9b89-08b73fc5e2db" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There is a local maximum at $x=-1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113767/selection/f6a9948068396877505e54f66bfd517f08252fd5fcfe067bfbdff6bc25b00dda" :answer/id #uuid "e704ba8e-dcd5-5574-a2d8-36d17ebaec0e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There is a local maximum at $x=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113767/selection/acc83c74f03748b2e35d16f5b1d34df696d35c1cc9214487ece6a3d7d0147310" :answer/id #uuid "5d480f4e-48c8-5c71-9e0b-81f67a7f49d1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There is a local minimum at $x=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113767/selection/8868f02215a5337e2a5129e75503fbec783bb64f355b374ffdd731bd6e356bdb" :answer/id #uuid "6771e68b-675d-55b3-b173-af4f1bdbdbe0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There is a local minimum at $x=-1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113767/selection/c112c665879534c7ad1d1042a8b28b7e5f3f70117c93b42716ffdd8ce86d808a" :answer/id #uuid "e9f3acf7-00e8-5f4b-8f46-c1c65af2fcc3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113767/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ec4dd24a-ba72-5563-bdc6-9ebd4dbae0dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-113767/selection/c112c665879534c7ad1d1042a8b28b7e5f3f70117c93b42716ffdd8ce86d808a" :answer-field/choices ["q-113767/selection/d9b70cb012b8a98348e0eb2b076c0ffaa03d2f9b5ed0b778c333cec60818df33" "q-113767/selection/f6a9948068396877505e54f66bfd517f08252fd5fcfe067bfbdff6bc25b00dda" "q-113767/selection/acc83c74f03748b2e35d16f5b1d34df696d35c1cc9214487ece6a3d7d0147310" "q-113767/selection/8868f02215a5337e2a5129e75503fbec783bb64f355b374ffdd731bd6e356bdb" "q-113767/selection/c112c665879534c7ad1d1042a8b28b7e5f3f70117c93b42716ffdd8ce86d808a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-113767" :question/id #uuid "5d605cec-3072-5304-adcf-70f0f4b40d1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-113767" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{e}^{x}},$ which of the following statements are true?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2x{e}^{x}-({x}^{2}+1){e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x+1}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{e}^{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{e}^{x}}\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that ${e}^{x}>0$ for all values of $x.$\n\nSo, $x=1$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{{e}^{0}}=-1<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =-\\frac{(2-1)^{2}}{{e}^{2}}=-{e}^{-2}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ |  | $↘$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=1.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=1$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nTherefore, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-113767/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff7c597d-d4a4-5567-add6-eac77475b0d6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-113767"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-113764/selection/acc83c74f03748b2e35d16f5b1d34df696d35c1cc9214487ece6a3d7d0147310" :answer/id #uuid "edce3891-628b-547a-bf0a-d2fb5e5d6d50" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There is a local minimum at $x=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113764/selection/3e861be81d35c8d4cfc14cc67d7919f2650d5e9bc654eb4705de4f11578bdcff" :answer/id #uuid "59fd53b8-bf05-559f-b595-63f13fdd2124" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There is a local minimum at $x=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113764/selection/f7e0800a377a7e10703ce82b063e43b5665422ee38a1af86d72cc36c4ff07857" :answer/id #uuid "11d8c5ac-17e2-590e-ac86-4d434e7e8e23" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There is a local maximum at $x=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113764/selection/c112c665879534c7ad1d1042a8b28b7e5f3f70117c93b42716ffdd8ce86d808a" :answer/id #uuid "ff9bd6f7-7638-5b29-9130-d85ab9c7ec72" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113764/selection/f6a9948068396877505e54f66bfd517f08252fd5fcfe067bfbdff6bc25b00dda" :answer/id #uuid "5925efbf-133a-55d9-9f09-52af7b3ca29d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There is a local maximum at $x=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113764/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9ff2fa8f-02a0-520c-8597-a4bc14f6368e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-113764/selection/c112c665879534c7ad1d1042a8b28b7e5f3f70117c93b42716ffdd8ce86d808a" :answer-field/choices ["q-113764/selection/acc83c74f03748b2e35d16f5b1d34df696d35c1cc9214487ece6a3d7d0147310" "q-113764/selection/3e861be81d35c8d4cfc14cc67d7919f2650d5e9bc654eb4705de4f11578bdcff" "q-113764/selection/f7e0800a377a7e10703ce82b063e43b5665422ee38a1af86d72cc36c4ff07857" "q-113764/selection/c112c665879534c7ad1d1042a8b28b7e5f3f70117c93b42716ffdd8ce86d808a" "q-113764/selection/f6a9948068396877505e54f66bfd517f08252fd5fcfe067bfbdff6bc25b00dda"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-113764" :question/id #uuid "78ca0287-8329-5ae9-a1b1-58cbe5481203" :question/math-academy-id "q-113764" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x)={x}^{9}-9,$ which of the following statements are true?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=9{x}^{8}.\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n9{x}^{8}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =9(-1)^{8}=9>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =9(1)^{8}=9>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=0$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-113764/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff7c597d-d4a4-5567-add6-eac77475b0d6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-113764"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
